Behavior of Power Prices- Implications for the 
Valuation and Hedging of Financial Contracts 
Abstract 
This paper contains an empirical analysis of electric power prices using data from 
twelve regional markets. A central feature of the paper is the explicit recognition that it is 
not possible to store power or carry a negative inventory. Our objective is to characterize 
and explain the high degree of autocorrelation and seasonality in power prices and address 
salient issues that are pertinent for the valuation and hedging of power based financial 
contracts. We show that price behavior changes with each regional market so that a firm 
that seek to value or hedge power based contracts must use instruments from the region in 
which it operates. 
3 
Behavior of Power Prices- Implications for the 
Valuation and Hedging of Financial Contracts 
1. Introduction 
This paper is concerned with the behavior of electric power prices. Deregulation in 
the electric power market (Appendix I has a brief description) has spurred the interest of 
financial market participants. Because power is virtually non-storable1, financial contracts 
on electric power present risks not encountered in other asset markets. This paper 
provides an insight into the unique behavior of power prices and relates these observations 
to the non-storability of power. An understanding of the stochastic behavior of power 
prices is central to models for valuation and hedging of financial claims on the commodity. 
Further, this topic is relevant in designing risk-sharing mechanisms amongst stakeholders 
and in making investment decisions in these markets. 
There exists a well-developed theory of storage and the behavior of commodity 
prices. Early on this theory was developed by Working (1948, 1949) and later extended by 
Deaton and Laroque (1992, 1996), Williams and Wright (1991) and Chambers and Bailey 
(1996). These papers recognize that carrying of an inventory by speculators may reduce 
the extent of price shocks in periods of high net-demand for a commodity. Recent 
research by Routledge, Seppi and Spatt (1998) characterizes the structure of forward 
commodity prices after explicitly incorporating storage features. In related work, 
Bessembinder and Lemmon (1998) model power prices when no storage is permitted and 
when some market participants are risk averse. We relate our study to this recent research 
4 
and draw several conclusions to explain salient observations regarding autocorrelation and 
seasonality in power prices. 
This paper provides atleast three contributions to literature. First, we provide 
evidence from twelve markets regarding the applicability of a simple time series model to 
explain price behavior. We find that models based on storage considerations provide 
valuable insights into mean reversion, seasonality and other salient observations in the 
power markets. Second, we show that the difficulty in storage causes a segmentation of 
the markets. We propose a test to compare the distribution of price shocks across markets 
and glean lessons for cross-hedging of claims in these segmented markets. Third, even 
though the nature of commodity price behavior has been studied (e.g., Brennan and 
Schwartz (1985), Gibson and Schwartz (1990), Coratzer and Scwartz (1994) and 
Bessembinder, Coughenour, Seguin and Smoller (1995) and most recently by Schwartz 
(1997)), to our knowledge this is the first paper to study the across-market properties of 
power prices. This market is identified as a growth area in terms of availability of newer 
financial products and a corresponding need for risk management. 
To achieve our objectives, we first present an underlying storage model in Section 
2. We then analyze survey data on the wholesale prices of Electric Power, compiled by 
the Energy Market Report (EMR). The data consists of daily high and low prices for 
twelve delivery points in the United States from January 1st, 1995 to June 1st, 1998 and 
broken into two series- (1) Off-Peak energy prices for power delivered for the first six and 
last two hours of the day and (2) Peak energy prices for power delivered during the 
remaining hours of the day. An autoregressive process describes the evolution of 
seasonally detrended Electric Power prices (Section 3 and 4). Parameter estimates for this 
5 
process and the estimated residuals (representing net-demand induced price shocks) are 
shown to be significantly different across the various markets. We analyze this price 
behavior across markets and seasons to cull out the impact of storability for the valuation 
and hedging of financial contracts. 
Second, a distribution-free test is constructed to compare the distribution of netdemand 
induced price shocks (Section 5). A first-order discrete-state discrete-time 
Markov Process is used and the asymptotic distribution under the null hypothesis is 
characterized. A pair wise cross-region comparison points to a segmentation in the power 
markets and a qualitatively different distribution of net-demand induced shocks. The 
analysis clarifies the pitfalls in cross-hedging of financial claims with contracts from other 
markets. We use our empirical results to draw inferences on the two topics of interestimplications 
of non-storability on price behavior and for the valuation and hedging of 
financial contracts. 
In summary, the paper is organized as follows: Section 2 has the theoretical 
background, Section 3 describes the data, Section 4 contains an empirical study of 
wholesale prices and implications, Section 5 constructs a test to compare price shocks 
across markets and Section 6 concludes. 
2. Theoretical Background 
This section provides a brief overview of the relationship between storage and 
prices. We begin with a specification for demand and supply and attribute fluctuations in 
prices to net-demand shocks (as in Deaton and Larouque (1996), Routledge, Seppi and 
Spatt (1998) and Bessembinder and Lemmon (1998)). Suppose at each date t =1,2… 
6 
