3. Data 
We now proceed to relate the framework in Section 2 to data from the power 
markets. Data for this study is obtained from the Energy Market Report Database on 
wholesale prices of Electric Power for the period January 1st, 1995 to June 1st, 1998 for 
twelve delivery points across the United States (Appendix II). Each day, the Energy 
Market Report (EMR) staff contacts participating subscribers in each region by phone. 
Due to a large number of participants, EMR obtains transaction price data on each day of 
the week without contacting the same sources every day (currently EMR contacts over 
seventy five different utilities, marketers and power brokers each week). In the average 
five-day week, each source is contacted three times. 
Prices in the database are the high and the low prices of transactions reported by 
the participants contacted on a particular day. Note that since power consumption and 
production are simultaneous, trades are struck for one day forward. A price contract on 
Monday is for energy that will be delivered on Tuesday. On Fridays, two sets of prices 
are reported- weekend prices and prices for the following Monday. In the event of 
government holidays, prices are reported at the earliest day possible, usually the day 
before the holiday. 
The database comprises daily high and low prices for twelve delivery points in the 
country for the period January 1st, 1995 to June 1st, 1998, broken into two series- (1) Off- 
8 
Peak energy prices for power delivered for the first six and last two hours of the day (10 
PM to 6 AM) and, (2) Peak energy prices for power delivered during remaining hours of 
the day (6 AM to 10 PM). Wholesale prices are reported in dollars per Megawatt-Hour. 
Transmission charges complicate the process of price reporting. For example, energy 
purchased in Northern California and exported to Southern California might be delivered 
at some additional cost. Suppose a power producer pays a charge for use of a transmission 
line, EMR reports transaction prices that include this charge. Hence prices are for a 
particular delivery point in each region. 
Table 1 has summary statistics for the entire data set. The data is categorized by 
delivery point and by Peak and Off-Peak prices. In each cell we report the daily mean and 
daily standard deviation of prices for each region. In general, Peak prices are higher than 
Off-Peak prices in each region. The average Peak prices range between $15.2 per 
Megawatt-Hour and $21.7 per Megawatt-hour while average Off-Peak prices range 
between $10.0 per Megawatt-Hour and $11.2 per Megawatt-hour. Further, average Peak 
prices are more volatile than Off-Peak prices. The daily standard deviation of Peak prices 
is between $5.4 and $9.3 while the daily standard deviation of Off-Peak prices is between 
$2.8 and $5.0. Note that there is a substantial variation in Peak prices across the twelve 
delivery points. For example, the average Peak price in the North-West Rockies is $15.2 
per Megawatt-Hour against $21.7 per Megawatt-Hour in Mead. 
The difference in the average price between Peak and Off-Peak hours serves as a 
first introduction to the unique nature of this market. Because power is not storable, 
excess supply, (gt −ct), in the Off-Peak hours is not easily stored for use during Peak 
hours. Such daily fluctuations are unlikely for a storable commodity because speculators 
9 
would store inventory to take advantage of deterministic price changes. For storable 
commodities, convenience yields or the cost of holding a commodity from one period to 
another is related to the wastage factor and to the interest rate. No such relationship is 
viable for a non-storable commodity. From a risk management perspective, Off-Peak 
power based financial contracts provide a different risk exposure than Peak power 
contracts. 
