The “seasonal means” specification is motivated by a number of reasons. In the 
nascent power market there is a paucity of data. A complicated parameterized process 
may “fit” the data well but provide little guidance on stability of the parameters or out-of- 
sample performance. For the seasonal means model used in this paper we could have used 
a dummy for every 15 days rather than for each month. A trade-off between the number of 
data points for each time period and the possibility of misspecification errors led to this 
choice. 
Note that seasonality accounts for a significant portion of the variation in monthly 
prices. The seasonal means for Peak and Off-Peak prices exhibit significant variation 
across the twelve months and across the delivery points. For example, June prices of 
Electric Power range between $5.7 and $7.4 per Megawatt-Hour for Off-Peak prices as 
against $11.4 to $18.5 per Megawatt-Hour for Peak prices. 
The seasonal variation in prices across regions is on account of two reasons – the 
inability to store power for later use and an inability to transmit power to other distant 
areas. In other words, the absence of storage may allow for systematic increase of prices 
11 
in certain months of the year because excess supply cannot be stored or transferred to the 
markets where there is a demand. Price shocks are not transmitted and equilibrium prices 
in one region are different from equilibrium prices in another region. 
