In the valuation of financial claims, a key input is the underlying stochastic process 
that describes changes in the commodity price. Evidence from each of the twelve markets 
examined here shows that it is important to use a data generating process and parameter 
estimates from data that corresponds to the particular market in which the agent operates2. 
Consider for example, claims valued in the NorthWest (NW) compared to Canada (CA) 
with an auto-regressive process parameter of 0.88 as against 0.78 that implies a sharper 
mean reversion in Canadian prices. Hence all else being the same, contingent claims that 
are out-of-the money are likely to be more valuable in NorthWest than in the Canadian 
market. This distinction is of importance across regions and also between Peak and Off- 
Peak prices. 
13 
Figure 3 is a histogram plot of errors ut,i,rfor one of the delivery points, for Peak 
power prices. Even though the autoregressive process captures a significant portion of the 
net demand related shocks, the residuals of the autoregressive process exhibit large 
changes characterized by excess kurtosis and skewness. Note that lagged correlations in 
the residuals do not exhibit any marked pattern and are in most cases less than 0.05 for 
lags greater than 1. The residuals of the estimated process also vary significantly across 
regions. This residual has important implications for risk management and for the pricing 
and hedging of claims. 
Explaining the residual component, ut,i,r , has belonged to a tradition in financial and 
economic research. It is plausible to model the error component or residual with either 
parametric or non-parametric procedures. Recent financial research has focused on using a 
combination of Gaussian errors and Poisson increments to capture features of the data 
generating process. Indeed, such a combination may be applied in our case (results not 
reported here). From the perspective of valuation of contracts and risk management, this 
indicates the differences in the nature of the risks in each of the markets. The standard 
deviation of these residual shocks range from a minimum of $2.16 to a maximum of $4.47. 
This difference significantly changes the assessment of the distribution of future prices of 
power, which is an input in the valuation and hedging of financial claims. Other 
distributional features are markedly different between the markets- the skewness and 
kurtosis across markets shows wide differences- that are again important for the purposes 
of risk management and valuation because they indicate that similar exposures can have 
varied risks inherent in a contract depending on the regional market. 
14 
The evidence outlined above illustrates the pitfalls in cross-hedging of financial 
claims. Shocks to prices in a particular market need not be transmitted to the other 
markets on account of an inability to store power or transmit it to distant regions. A check 
of across-market correlation of residuals reveals that they are near zero in many cases 
(Table 6). In some of the neighbouring markets this correlation is marginally positive but 
the hedges may not work well. A large price jump in, for example, California will mildly, if 
at all, effect demand in Nevada because of the limited capacity of lines between the two 
markets. In fact if there is an excess supply of power in Nevada, there may be a minimal 
impact on the prices in Nevada even though prices are soaring in California! Such muted 
impacts across markets are often observed in the case of capital markets that have barriers 
to international capital flows (or are delinked). 
Because markets for power clear continuously, power must be produced and 
consumed at the same instant. Excess demand shocks cannot be catered to by stored 
resources since there is a lag between starting a new plant and bringing it on line. We may 
expect that areas that have high transmission capacity can transmit excess power to 
neighboring areas, thus equilibrating demand and supply. Such integration is not possible if 
there are bottlenecks or an inability to transmit power over long distances due to 
transmission losses. The excess kurtosis and skewness probably relates to increased 
demand during certain days of the year when regional demand cannot be satisfied due to 
segmentation of the markets. 
5. Across Market Comparison of Net Demand Related Shocks 
15 
In this section we construct a non-parametric test for the across-market 
comparison of net-demand related price shocks. This methodology compares the entire 
distribution of the demand shocks in each region. For example, if two random variables 
are perfectly correlated but the distribution of one has larger tails than the other, this test 
will show that the two distributions are different. This distinction is of importance from a 
risk management perspective. We wish to compare the across-market distributions of 
detrended residuals Xt,i,r , which relates to the non-deterministic part of net-demand. A 
distribution-free test with a first order discrete-state discrete-time Markov Process is 
convenient for such an across region comparison test (Routledge, Seppi and Spatt (1998) 
construct a similar two state example). For our across-region comparison the residuals 
