Tables 4 has estimates of the transition matrices for Four Corners, one of the 
twelve regions over the sample period for both Peak and non-Peak power. Such a 
transition matrix is useful for several reasons. The matrix depicts the net-demand induced 
price shock in one period conditional on the shock in the previous period. For example in 
Table 4, if the price of power is $8 above its seasonal mean in a particular period, there is 
a 0.68 empirical probability that the price will remain at this level in the following period. 
From the perspective of risk management, this gives a clear idea about the persistence of 
17 
net-demand induced price shocks and the probability of prices reverting to the mean 
values in the following period. 
Table 5 compares these transition matrices for each market (power hub) in our 
sample. The table reports chi-square test statistics when a particular region is the null 
matrix and is compared to each one of the other eleven regions. A five percent confidence 
level for ninety degrees of freedom gives a value of 69.13. A null hypothesis of equality is 
rejected in most for transition matrices of Peak power prices. However we cannot reject 
the null hypothesis of equality of transition matrices for many of the regions for Off-Peak 
power prices. Note that the results do not change as the partitions of the matrix are 
changed. 
The analysis shows that residual distributions representing net demand shocks are 
significantly different in each region and therefore the associated risks of operating in each 
region are also different. The issue of market segmentation is important for several other 
reasons too. First, from an economic standpoint an integrated market helps to equilibrate 
demand and supply by sending excess resources to markets where they are in short supply. 
This integration in turn influences the behavior of prices and consequently the prices of 
financial asset and contingent claims. If agents are risk averse, a decrease in volatility of 
prices is likely to increase agent welfare. 
As noted above, the chi-square test statistic of equality is rejected in most for 
transition matrices of Peak power prices. However we cannot reject the null hypothesis of 
equality of transition matrices for many of the regions for Off-Peak power prices. It is 
important to recognize the reason for this result. Since there is an excess supply of power 
18 
during Off-Peak hours, shocks to prices are not large but during Peak hours “excess” 
demand leads to jumps in prices as prices change to equilibrate demand and supply. 
The power markets thus provide a unique setting to study the implications of nonstorability 
for price behavior and the pricing and hedging of financial assets. An important 
implication of storability is that the temporal and across market segmentation leads to a 
greater volatility in prices. Financial assets must therefore be valued taking into account 
the market characteristics of price behavior where a financial contract is valued. 
6. Concluding Remarks 
A simple framework is applied to study price behavior in the power markets. For 
our analysis, we use survey data from twelve markets and empirically analyze the behavior 
of prices. We relate many features of price behavior to the non-storability of power. It is 
argued that the behavior of power prices – deterministic changes in mean prices, 
seasonality and systematic increases in conditional volatility -is partly on account of the 
non-storability of power. Since the net-demand related shocks are shown to be different in 
each market, the associated risks change with the market in which a firm is operating. 
Storability influences the temporal behavior of prices which in turn is of utmost 
importance in the valuation and hedging of financial claims. Because markets for power 
clear continuously and are temporally independent, prices in one period may not influence 
prices in another period. Further, because price behavior in a market changes with regions 
(a segmented market), a firm that seeks to value or hedge financial contracts on power 
must use derivative instruments that are based on prices in the region that it operates. This 
market serves as a useful real-world laboratory to examine the implication of storability 
19 
and its lessons for risk management. With regard to firms that operate in these markets, 
cross-hedging of financial contracts through exchange traded futures may be problematic 
unless the firms operate in or near the region on which the exchange traded contract is 
based. 

