1. Some imperfect storage mediums are available. For example, a dam can store water 
that is converted to power or energy may be stored in the form of nuclear stock piles. 
Even though these storage avenues are available it takes several hours to start a power 
unit that converts the energy into usable power. Therefore the capacity to quickly 
convert stored energy into electric power remains limited. Further startup costs of 
$1000 and upwards are incurred on each startup and maintenance costs of $400 per 
day are added on. 
2. Our analysis proceeds under the true measure as opposed to a risk neutral measure. 
The relationship of contingent claim prices and asset prices is described in finance 
theory. In the theory of valuation, an agent typically assigns a price to each marginal 
unit of consumption received at some future date in a particular state of the world. In 
equilibrium, there exists a positive pricing operator p t  such that for any contingent 
payoff Z (Pt) the current value of the security is given by: C(t)=E[Z(PT)pT], 
where C(t) the derivative or contract price at time t and the expectation is under the 
true measure. In the Finance literature, the pricing operator p t has been expressed as 
a function of the aggregate consumption and a risk aversion parameter g ( e.g, 
Rubinstein (1976) uses a constant proportional risk aversion specfication, i.e.                                                             
