APPENDIX I: Overview of the Power Market 
Electric Power is a homogenous good whose physical characteristics are the same regardless of 
when and where it is produced or consumed. Electric Power has unique features- it is almost 
impossible to store power and the generation and use of power must be simultaneous. Since 
Electric Power is a completely perishable resource, there is no possibility of making use of 
productive capacity in hours when demand for power may be substantially less than supply 
(some imperfect storage media are available). The quantity of power consumed per unit of time 
is measured in Mega-Watt-Hours (one Mega-Watt of energy delivered for a period of one 
hour). 
From a physical perspective, the United States (combined with portions of Mexico and 
Canada) comprises three major power transmission networks. Power hubs serve as 
interconnections between networks serving different geographical areas (our data set includes 
price data from these hubs). The three networks are further divided into one hundred and forty 
“control areas” where an individual called the “control operator” ensures a balance between 
demand and supply of power. These control areas can be regarded as segments of the three 
networks where utilities or power pools are responsible towards their constituent members. 
One of the institutional arrangements in the power industry is in the form of “power 
pools”. The purpose of power pools is to facilitate power transactions amongst its participating 
members. In other words, demand and supply are first matched within the power pool and any 
excess power is sold to outside parties at market prices. It is this “excess power” that 
constitutes the wholesale power market. 
Excess power is transmitted to other control areas and utilities through power hubs and 
associated transmission lines (Appendix II has details of twelve hubs for which price data is 
analyzed in this paper). The generation and transmission entity serving a particular region or 
34 
country typically transfers power to one or more distribution entities, that are then responsible 
for distributing electricity to retail customers in specific geographic areas that they serve 
exclusively. The inability to store and transport power and the physical barrier in terms of 
transmission capacity is responsible for segmenting the markets . 
35 
APPENDIX II : Regions covered by the Data Set. 
Currently the EMR collects prices in twelve distinct markets.                                        
•  Canada (CA) - Prices reported at the Canada and United States border.                                                     
•  Northwest and North Rockies (NW)- Includes Washington, Oregon, Idaho, and Montana.                                                                  
•  Mid-Columbia (MC) - The market corresponding to the region of Central Washington State 
as well as Northern Oregon between the Grand Coulee Dam the McNary Dam.                                                            
•  California Oregon Border and Nevada Oregon Borders (COB).                                              
•  Northern California (NC)- The northern portion of the State above Midway.                                                       
•  Midway and Sylmar (MW)- located between Northern and Southern California.                                                        
•  Southern California (SC)- The southern portion of the State below Midway.                                                       
•  Mead (MD)- the Southern Nevada Market.                              
•  Palo Verde (PV)- located in Southern Arizona.                                
•  Inland Southwest (IN)- Consisting of Southern Nevada, Arizona, New Mexico, and 
Western Texas.         
•  Four-Corners (FC)- At the Utah, Arizona, New Mexico and Colorado border.                                                          
•  Central Rockies (CR)- Northern Nevada, Utah and Colorado. 
Note that some of these data collection points represent actual purchasers and producers of 
energy while other locations, particularly Midway and Sylmar, Mead, Palo Verde, FourCorners, 
as well as the Mid-Columbia, are often transaction points for energy that is ultimately 
used elsewhere. These transaction points exist for the most part where many transmission lines 
converge and facilitate power transfers from one system to another. 